ess / Oxford University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.48,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\"","academic press signal: Oxford University Press / Oxford University Press"]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"argues\""]},{"id":"essayistic","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"criticism\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Blackface minstrelsy both embodied and disrupted racial tendencies in largely white, male working-class audiences, transgressing the color line while enabling a self-consciously white working class through a dialectic of “love and theft.”","confidence":0.64},"academicOrientation":{"score":70,"confidence":0.6},"profileConfidence":0.74,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"61398e2c0a06ce8291d4f4bde7da939b7480ba9f7c6f9c8c50bc1508e39b4713","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"For over two centuries, America has celebrated the same African-American culture it attempts to control and repress, and nowhere is this phenomenon more apparent than in the strange practice of blackface performance. Born of extreme racial and class conflicts, the blackface minstrel show appropriated black dialect, music, and dance; at once applauded and lampooned black culture; and, ironically, contributed to a \"blackening of America.\" Drawing on recent research in cultural studies and social history, Eric Lott examines the role of the blackface minstrel show in the political struggles of the years leading up to the Civil War. Reading minstrel music, lyrics, jokes, burlesque skits, and illustrations in tandem with working-class racial ideologies and the sex/gender system, Love and Theft argues that blackface minstrelsy both embodied and disrupted the racial tendencies of its largely white, male, working-class audiences. Underwritten by envy as well as repulsion, sympathetic identification as well as fear--a dialectic of \"love and theft\"--the minstrel show continually transgressed the color line even as it enabled the formation of a self-consciously white working class.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lott"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Minstrel shows","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lott"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Race relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lott"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"United States Civil War, 1861-1865","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lott"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Working class","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lott"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"United states, race relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lott"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Working class, united states","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lott"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"United states, history, civil war, 1861-1865","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-love-and-theft-blackface-minstrelsy-and-the-american-working-class-eric-lot